<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-08" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Week 8</title>

<introduction>
    <p>
        This is an outline of the topics we covered in Week 8 of the course, from October 19 to October 24.
    </p>
</introduction>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Monday 10/19</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Compute derivatives involving natural log using implicit differentiation.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Explore applications of implicit differentiation. 
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Applications of Implicit Differentiation</title>

        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find all points on the graph of 
                    <md>
                        x^2 + 2y^2 = 9
                    </md> where the tangent line goes through the point <m>(5,1)</m>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Derivatives of Inverse Functions</title>
            
<p component="b" workspace="4in">
    Another main application of implicit differentiation is to finding the derivatives of inverse functions.
</p>
<p component="a">
    Another main application of implicit differentiation is to finding the derivatives of inverse functions.
</p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            So how can we actually use this?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            So how can we actually use this? 
        </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the derivative of <md>
                        f(x) = \log_b(x)
                    </md> for any <m>b</m>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the derivative of <md>
                        f(x) = \ln(x^2 + 2x^2 + 5)
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the derivative of <md>
                        f(x) = \ln(x^2 + 2x^2 + 5)
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the derivative of <md>
                        f(x) = \ln(x^{1/3})
                    </md> and <md>
                        g(x) = (\ln(x))^{1/3}
                    </md>.
                    
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the derivative of <md>
                        f(x) = \ln\left(\frac{(x+3)^2}{\sqrt{x^2 + 4}}\right)
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Inverse Trigonometric Functions</title>
            
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                The other kind of function we can apply this to is inverse trigonometric functions.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                The other kind of function we can apply this to is inverse trigonometric functions. 
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
</handout>


</section>